   same way by both wkhtmltopdf and a browser's own print/PDF engine, so
   this isn't tied to whichever one actually renders the final PDF.
   thead repeating on every page a table spans is native wkhtmltopdf/print
   behaviour already, as long as nothing here fights it - table-header-
   group makes that explicit rather than relying on the default. */
.pop-items-table thead {
  display: table-header-group;
}

.pop-items-table tr {
  page-break-inside: avoid;
}

/* Always bottom-pinned, on whatever page it ends up on - not just "don't
   split this block". Its own height comes from an inline style (see
   get_purchase_order_print_context's _estimate_po_sign_block_placement
   in jinja.py): however much space is actually left on the CURRENT page
   below the totals block, so vertical-align:bottom on the inner div
   below pushes it down to the true bottom of that leftover space.
   Two other approaches were tried and reverted after failing on a real
   wkhtmltopdf render: a 2-row "sticky footer" table sized to one page's
   height (row 2 landed at the TOP of an accidental extra page instead of
   the bottom, once row 1's real content pushed the table even slightly
   past one page), and position:fixed (took the block out of normal
   flow entirely, so nothing reserved space for it and it overlapped the
   totals block instead of sitting below it). This estimate-based
   approach is less elegant but is the one actually verified working
   across single-page and multi-page real PDF output.
   Table, not flexbox - see the note further up this file for why. */
.pop-sign-block {
  page-break-inside: avoid;
  display: table;
  width: 100%;
}

.pop-sign-block-inner {
  display: table-cell;
  vertical-align: bottom;
}

/* Only added when the estimate above says the block wouldn't fit in
   what's left on the current page - forces a fresh page first so the
   inline height (now sized to nearly that whole fresh page, not just a
   leftover sliver) has a genuinely empty page to pin itself against. */
